  "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    We present the first implementation of an X-ray phase-contrast imaging approach using single 2D-grating phase retrieval and a quasi-monochromatic hard K\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\alpha$$</jats:tex-math>\n                    </jats:inline-formula>\n                    X-ray source generated\n                    <jats:italic>via</jats:italic>\n                    high-intensity femtosecond laser—plasma interaction. To highlight its pertinence, we demonstrate quantitative phase-contrast measurements of a known canonical physical object, retrieving phase distribution with high-fidelity, under varying experimental conditions. As a marker of such performance, a correlation score of up to 0.922 ± 0.008 is obtained between theoretical and experimental phase images. The consistency of the retrieved phase gradients is further quantitatively evaluated using the Confidence Map, a self-consistency metric derived directly from the acquired data. This framework supports a systematic parametric study aimed at quantifying the influence of X-ray source characteristics on phase retrieval and thus optimizing imaging performance by balancing exposure time and spatial resolution. Two operating regimes are investigated: fixed integrated photon-count conditions with varying spatial coherence of the X-ray source, and fixed acquisition-time conditions with varying X-ray photon flux. This work establishes a pathway toward minimizing exposure times while preserving accurate phase retrieval, with the long-term objective of enabling short-time exposure of time-resolved phase-contrast imaging.\n                  </jats:p>",
